  "title": "Am ersten Mai",
  "subtitle": "Adjective endings in the dative, and ordinals",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "type": "overview",
      "label": "Überblick",
      "title": "Lesson intro",
      "minutes": 40,
      "learn": [
        "In the dative every adjective ends in -en, with or without an article.",
        "mit dem starken Schmerz, mit einem starken Schmerz, mit der neuen Pflegekraft, mit einer neuen Pflegekraft.",
        "If the article already ends in -n or -m, the adjective is -en. Feminine dative der and einer do not end in -n or -m, and the adjective is still -en.",
        "Ordinals decline: am dritten Tag, am ersten Mai, nach der zweiten Dosis."
      ],
      "vocab": [
        {
          "de": "Patient",
          "article": "der",
          "plural": "die Patienten",
          "en": "patient",
          "emoji": "🤒",
          "glossaryKey": "patient"
        },
        {
          "de": "Kollegin",
          "article": "die",
          "plural": "die Kolleginnen",
          "en": "colleague",
          "emoji": "👩‍⚕️",
          "glossaryKey": "kollegin"
        },
        {
          "de": "Tag",
          "article": "der",
          "plural": "die Tage",
          "en": "day",
          "emoji": "📅",
          "glossaryKey": "tag"
        },
        {
          "de": "Dosis",
          "article": "die",
          "plural": "die Dosen",
          "en": "dose",
          "emoji": "💊",
          "glossaryKey": "dosis"
        },
        {
          "de": "Verband",
          "article": "der",
          "plural": "die Verbände",
          "en": "dressing",
          "emoji": "🩹",
          "glossaryKey": "verband"
        },
        {
          "de": "Zimmer",
          "article": "das",
          "plural": "die Zimmer",
          "en": "room",
          "emoji": "🚪",
          "glossaryKey": "zimmer"
        }
      ],
      "text": {
        "title": "Am dritten Tag",
        "de": "Die Ärztin spricht mit dem neuen Patienten. Die Pflegekraft kommt mit einer neuen Kollegin. Am dritten postoperativen Tag prüft sie den Verband. Nach der zweiten Dosis bleibt der Schmerz leicht. Der Patient liegt in dem kleinen Zimmer.",
        "en": "The doctor speaks with the new patient. The nurse comes with a new colleague. On the third postoperative day she checks the dressing. After the second dose the pain stays mild. The patient is lying in the small room."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "type": "grammar",
      "label": "Grammar",
      "title": "Dative: the adjective ends in -en",
      "practice": "choose",
      "heroTitle": "Nominative, accusative, dative",
      "heroImage": "clipboard",
      "story": [
        "Mit <b>dem neuen Patienten</b> and mit <b>einer neuen Kollegin</b> are dative. Both adjectives end in -en.",
        "<b>Am dritten Tag</b> is an + dem. The ordinal declines like an adjective."
      ],
      "questionRule": "Dative adjective = <b>-en</b>, always. Plural dative nouns also take -n where they can: den neuen Verbänden.",
      "tables": [
        {
          "title": "Dativ",
          "caption": "Every adjective in this table ends in -en.",
          "columns": [
            "Maskulin",
            "Neutrum",
            "Feminin",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Dat der",
              "cells": [
                "dem starken Schmerz",
                "dem kleinen Pflaster",
                "der neuen Pflegekraft",
                "den neuen Verbänden"
              ],
              "highlight": true
            },
            {
              "label": "Dat ein",
              "cells": [
                "einem starken Schmerz",
                "einem kleinen Pflaster",
                "einer neuen Pflegekraft",
                "keinen neuen Verbänden"
              ],
              "highlight": true
            },
            {
              "label": "ohne Artikel",
              "cells": [
                "mit starkem Schmerz",
                "mit kleinem Pflaster",
                "mit neuer Pflegekraft",
                "mit neuen Verbänden"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Ordinalzahlen",
          "caption": "am = an + dem. The ordinal takes the dative ending.",
          "columns": [
            "Formel",
            "Beispiel",
            "Kasus"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "dritte",
              "cells": [
                "am dritten Tag",
                "am dritten postoperativen Tag",
                "Dativ"
              ],
              "highlight": true
            },
            {
              "label": "erste",
              "cells": [
                "am ersten Mai",
                "am ersten Tag",
                "Dativ"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "zweite",
              "cells": [
                "nach der zweiten Dosis",
                "nach der zweiten Visite",
                "Dativ"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "warning": {
        "title": "Der ist nicht immer Nominativ",
        "items": [
          {
            "de": "mit der neuen Pflegekraft",
            "en": "Feminine dative. der looks like masculine nominative, but the adjective is -en.",
            "tone": "ok"
          },
          {
            "de": "mit der neue Pflegekraft",
            "en": "Dative adjectives do not stay on -e.",
            "tone": "stop"
          },
          {
            "de": "mit neuen Verbänden",
            "en": "No article, and the adjective is still -en. The noun adds -n.",
            "tone": "ok"
          },
          {
            "de": "mit neue Verbände",
            "en": "Bare dative plural is not the base form.",
            "tone": "stop"
          }
        ],
        "tip": "ohne Artikel in the masculine or neuter dative is -em, not -en: mit starkem Schmerz. The -en rule is for an article that is already there, and for every plural."
      },
      "tips": [
        {
          "title": "-n or -m, then -en",
          "body": "den, dem, einen and einem already end in -n or -m, so the adjective is -en. In the dative, der and einer are the exceptions to that shortcut: they do not end in -n or -m, and the adjective is still -en. Without an article, masculine and neuter take -em (mit starkem Schmerz) and feminine takes -er (mit neuer Pflegekraft). Plural without an article is still -en.",
          "mnemonic": "Dativ: Adjektiv auf -en. Ohne Artikel maskulin: -em.",
          "emoji": "3️⃣"
        }
      ],
